{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792931759,"nonce":"Sx05gBFZOBEgBHR76eIMbhqlFmY=","title":"Tiered: with LLVM on, tier-0 code is never counter-vectorized, so short kernels run scalar until LLVM lands","message":"`run_interp_safe_opts_cached_for_cranelift` (lib.rs) selects fixed-width counter vectors only when Cranelift is the final tier. With LLVM as tier 2 the shared optimised body keeps scalar reductions for LLVM's own vectorizer, and the Cranelift baseline compiles that same body, so until the LLVM install lands a loop runs scalar and also pays its OSR count, compare, slot load and branch per iteration. The body is shared on purpose: the interpreter, the baseline and the LLVM resume points read one frame layout.\n\nzylua evidence (Mac, release c269a273, ZYLUA_LLVM=1, per repetition in one process): lambda_call 8.4 to 11 ms on the first repetition, then 1.2 ms; Cranelift-only every repetition is 1.4 ms. Whole elapsed LLVM vs Cranelift-only: lambda_call 8.9 vs 1.4 ms, closure_call 9.3 vs 6.2 (LuaJIT 3.2, 4.9). The CLIF of lambda's main is an i64x2 loop unrolled 8x without LLVM and a scalar loop with probes with it. NUC, first repetition then steady (LuaJIT): nbody 105 then 54 ms (65), mandelbrot 31 then 10 (17), op_overload 64 then 34 (10.5); steady state already beats LuaJIT on nbody and mandelbrot, so their CI gap is this plus the promotion order (98b90bc: nbody's run-once chunk and three library functions promote before NBody:advance, which gets LLVM code at 88 to 110 ms).\n\nOptions: compile the baseline from a vectorized copy and give it resume points of its own layout (frames leave it only into its own code or at loop exit); or make the LLVM tier start from the vectorized body; or order promotions by heat and leave run-once chunk regions whose loops only call for last.","files":null}]}